This repository is two kinds of work and carries two licences, one suited to
each. Which applies is decided by the file, not by where you got it.

  Designs and documentation                      CC BY 4.0
    Every .scad source, its generated .stl and   LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt
    .png, every Markdown document including
    READMEs and TODOs, and any other source or
    generated artifact that describes or builds
    a printable object.

  Software and repository tooling                MIT
    Everything under scripts/, tests/, and       LICENSES/MIT.txt
    .vscode/, plus repository configuration such
    as .gitignore.

These lists are inclusive rather than exhaustive: a new file takes the licence
of the kind of work it contains, not an accidental default based on an extension
that was not named here. A per-file SPDX-License-Identifier controls whenever
one is present. Where a generated file cannot carry one — .stl and .png, for
example — it takes the licence of the source it was generated from, which is
CC BY 4.0 for the printable objects in this repository.

Why two.

Creative Commons advises against applying CC licences to software: they have no
source-versus-binary provisions, no requirement to supply source, and CC 4.0
expressly declines to license patent rights (section 2(b)(1)), where MIT is
merely silent on them. So the Python does not belong under CC BY.

The reverse is just as true. MIT grants permission "to deal in the Software",
which is the wrong noun for an STL, and its attribution requirement is thinner
than these designs' upstream demands. So the designs do not belong under MIT.

Putting the designs under CC BY 4.0 also makes them the same licence as what
they build on, which removes a step of reasoning rather than adding one.

Upstream attribution.

The openGrid snap profile reproduced in opengrid_magnet_snap/ is not original to
this repository. It derives from openGrid by David D
(https://www.opengrid.world), CC BY 4.0, by way of mitufy's openGrid projects
(https://github.com/mitufy/opengrid-projects), also CC BY 4.0. Those files are
CC BY 4.0 in and CC BY 4.0 out. The attribution and the statement of
modification carried in each affected file's header are licence conditions, not
courtesies, and must travel with the profile.

Nothing here is derived from QuackWorks (https://github.com/AndyLevesque/
QuackWorks), which is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Parts here are designed to sit alongside
its Underware cable channels, which is use rather than derivation. See
OPENGRID.md for why that distinction is worth keeping.
